Mars its ancient crust is pockmarked  with craters craps and mounds they tell  the story of its past remnants of a  violent history some were formed by  impacts materials from space smashing  into the surface and leaving behind huge  craters others formed through erosion  and some like the monumental Olympus  Mons three times taller than Everest are  massive volcanoes but recently  scientists have found evidence of  volcanism on an even greater scale the  biggest explosions in the red planet's  history  we know that Mars has been modified by  volcanic eruptions throughout its  history but puzzlingly when we look at  its surface there are fewer volcanic  structures than we'd expect to  scientists turn their attention to the  Arabia terror a region of ancient crust  heavily scarred with impact craters it  was not however thought to be volcanic  one crater in particular caught their  eye  Eden Patera it didn't seem to fit with  their expectations for an impact crater  lacking defining features like a raised  rim a central Mound or a defined ejecta  pattern instead Eden Vitara bore a  striking resemblance to a structure seen  on earth called a caldera and calderas a  volcanic when you think of a volcano you  probably imagine a cone-shaped Mountain  spewing out lava and ash but if enough  material is ejected the volcano can  collapse in on itself forming a caldera  like this one the scientists think  they're looking at something similar on  Mars calderas are usually surrounded by  mounds of material boost out when the  volcano erupted but we don't see this  around eden Patera instead of oozing out  magma eden Patera must have exploded  with unimaginable force hurling billions  of tons of material thousands of  kilometres away and high into the  atmosphere spreading it across the whole  planet if this was a volcano it wasn't  just any volcano it was a supervolcano  this eruption of Mount st. Helens on  earth in 1980 ejected just over one  cubic kilometer of material super  volcanoes are capable of explosive  eruptions over a thousand times larger  the scientists discovered three  potential sites of extinct super  volcanoes but it's hard to know for sure  these volcanoes would have erupted over  three and a half billion years ago plus  super volcanic eruptions are so  devastating they destroy but of the  evidence that smaller volcanoes leave  behind and yet whilst hard to identify  the impact of super volcanic eruptions  is dramatic we know that in Earth's  history super volcanoes modified the  geology of entire continents and the  climate of the whole globe if there were  super volcanoes on Mars it could  revolutionize our understanding of the  early Martian atmosphere and explain  some of the big mysteries of Martian  geology like this bizarre region of  fretted terrain near the Arabia Tara the  scientists who did this research believe  that with more investigation we could  find more super volcanoes posing as  impact craters on the surface of the Red  Planet  but either way it seems that Mars is  ancient pastures even more violent than  we once thought  you
 

